Cache behavior depends on the history of memory references, so timing an instruction in isolation does not give its execution cost in a program. For a hard real-time bound, observations from sampled runs cannot establish that an access is always a hit. Alt, Ferdinand, Martin, and Wilhelm's \emph{Cache Behavior Prediction by Abstract Interpretation} instead tracks information that holds for every feasible access history. Daniel Kaestner's \emph{Safe Worst-Case Execution Time Analysis by Abstract Interpretation of Executable Code} places that cache analysis inside an executable-level calculation that also accounts for other hardware effects and control flow.

\section{From Concrete Accesses to Abstract Ages}

Assume an $A$-way set-associative cache with least-recently-used replacement, no interference, and known set indices. In each set a concrete cache state is an ordered list of distinct blocks, most recent first. Accessing a block moves it to the front or inserts it there, dropping the last element if capacity would be exceeded. A resident has age equal to its zero-based position; give a nonresident the sentinel age $A$. Sets unaffected by the access do not change. Apply this model to blocks, not to byte addresses: addresses on the same line are the same block for this transfer.

A must analysis may store an upper bound on each block's age across all represented histories; a may analysis may store a lower bound. At a join, take componentwise maxima in the first analysis and minima in the second. An upper bound strictly below $A$ proves a hit; a lower bound equal to $A$ proves a miss. The two tests leave a third case, \emph{unclassified}, where neither proof works. For a simple must transfer, set the accessed block's bound to zero and increment all other bounds in that set, capped at $A$. This is sound because any other block ages by at most one; sharper transfers can sometimes show that a resident older than the accessed block does not age. A simple may transfer can set the accessed block's bound to zero and leave the other lower bounds unchanged, since the other ages cannot decrease. For an access specified by a set of possible blocks, apply the transfer for each possible choice and join. Using only the most likely choice would give a prediction rather than a bound.

\section{A Two-Way Cache by Hand}

\Cref{fig:visual-212} compares the two concrete cache lists just before
the last access in the first version of the fragment.

