\section{Bound the Cost Over Edge Counts}

Local classifications do not by themselves say how many times
an access executes. To turn the analysis into a regional bound,
take a single-entry, single-exit loop with a proved maximum trip
count and safe per-traversal edge costs. Use nonnegative integer
edge counts, a synthetic entry arc and exit arc, and flow
conservation at every real block. Assume the cycle model is
additive for this fragment and that no other cycles are present.
Every real trace must satisfy the constraints, but some solutions
may represent infeasible histories.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Loop CFG $G$, edge costs $c$, trip limit $K$
\ForAll{edges $e$}
  \State Declare integer $n_e\ge 0$
\EndFor
\State Set entry and exit arc counts to one
\To{Every visit must be accounted for on both sides.}
\ForAll{real blocks $v$}
  \State Require $\sum_{e\to v}n_e=\sum_{v\to e}n_e$
\EndFor
\To{Use a proved bound, not a measured trip count.}
\State Require $n_{\mathrm{body}}\le K$
\State Maximize $\sum_e c_e n_e$ subject to these constraints
\State \Return a certified upper bound on that maximum
\end{sudocode}

For a header that branches either to one body block or to the
exit, set $K=3$. Suppose each header-to-body edge costs $1$,
each body-to-header edge costs at most $7$ including its memory
access, and exiting the header costs $2$. Charge zero for the
synthetic arcs. The counts of the two loop edges agree, so the
objective is $8n_{\mathrm{body}}+2$. The maximum is $26$;
zero trips cost $2$. A calculation charging just three header
exits or omitting the final check will get the wrong result
even if every individual access cost is sound.

One invariant is that the cost of any concrete trace is at most
the objective evaluated at its edge counts. Another is
that such a count vector is feasible, which depends on the
justification of the loop limit. Without the cap this example
has an unbounded objective. In a solver with a time limit,
the best feasible solution is a lower bound on the maximization
objective and is not a safe result; use a justified upper bound
or report that no finite bound was obtained.

\section{Turning Local Facts into a Timing Bound}

Suppose all three accesses in the original fragment have serviced hit time $1$ cycle and a miss adds $8$, each access runs to completion before the next, and branch selection costs at most $2$. Both possible middle blocks are cold, so a bound is $3+2\cdot8+2=21$ cycles. Treating all accesses as misses would give $29$. In a loop, multiplying a cheap local cost by an assumed trip count is not sufficient: count bounds must be justified, and edge counts must satisfy control-flow conservation with the correct entry and exit counts. One approach, as in Kaestner's account, maximizes the sum of abstract block or edge costs times their execution counts subject to integer flow constraints. Incompatible paths left in that calculation may make the result larger, but excluding feasible paths can make it unsound.

Real pipelines can overlap accesses, and replacing a cache outcome with an isolated maximum latency does not justify a bound for a machine with timing anomalies. The inequalities must cover the modeled pipeline and memory subsystem, including any allowed preemption, interference, or initial states. The dossier's \emph{Time-Aware IR} treats temporal affinity as a profile-based optimization hint. It can suggest new layouts, but changing layout can change set mappings; a timing claim must be re-established for the resulting executable and hardware assumptions.
